## 📌 8. Difference Between >> and >>>

| `>>` | `>>>` |
|---|---|
| Signed right shift | Unsigned right shift |
| Preserves the sign | Fills empty left positions with zeros |
| Negative values remain negative after a right shift | A negative value shifted right can become a large positive value |
| For positive values, fills left positions with zeros | Always fills left positions with zeros |

Example:

```java
System.out.println(-8 >> 1);
System.out.println(-8 >>> 1);
```

Output:

```text
-4
2147483644
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 📌 9. Important Rules and Common Mistakes ⚠️

✅ Shift operators work with integer types such as `byte`, `short`, `char`, `int`, and `long`. Smaller integer types are promoted to `int` before the shift operation.

✅ For `int`, only the lowest 5 bits of the shift distance are used.

✅ For `long`, only the lowest 6 bits of the shift distance are used.

❌ Do not assume every left shift is safe multiplication. Overflow can change the result.

❌ Do not assume `>>` and `>>>` always produce the same result. They differ for negative numbers.

❌ Do not think `>>>` means division by 2. For negative numbers, its zero-filling behavior can produce a very large positive value.

